  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does the outer core make a magnetic field?",
      "a": "The liquid iron in the outer core flows because of heat, and the Earth's spin twists it into swirls. Moving metal carries electric currents, and those currents make a magnetic field that keeps itself going, like a dynamo.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the geodynamo?",
        "Why is the Earth a magnet?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a compass point north?",
      "a": "A compass needle is a small magnet free to turn. It lines up with the Earth's magnetic field, so one end points towards magnetic north, which is close to, but not exactly at, the North Pole.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a compass work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the magnetic field protect us from?",
      "a": "It deflects most of the charged particles streaming from the Sun, called the solar wind, which helps protect our atmosphere. When some particles slip in near the poles, they make the glowing auroras.",
      "asks": [
        "What causes the northern lights?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Does magnetic north move?",
      "a": "Yes. Because the iron in the core keeps flowing, magnetic north wanders, in recent decades by up to about 50 kilometres a year. Over long times the whole field even flips, swapping north and south.",
      "asks": [
        "Can the magnetic poles flip?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is there a giant magnet inside the Earth?",
      "a": "No. The core is far too hot for a permanent magnet, which loses its magnetism when heated. The field is made by moving liquid iron carrying electric currents.",
      "asks": [
        "Is the core a bar magnet?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The Earth's magnetic field has reversed many times; the last full reversal was about 780,000 years ago.",
    "Auroras happen when charged particles from the Sun hit the upper atmosphere near the magnetic poles."
  ],
